FINAL: Dune is a 1965 science fiction novel by American author Frank Herbert, originally published in two parts in Analog magazine (1963–1965) before being released by Chilton Books. It won the inaugural Nebula Award for Best Novel in 1965 and shared the Hugo Award in 1966, and is widely cited as the best-selling science fiction novel in history. Set in the distant future amidst a sprawling interstellar empire, the novel explores the complex interactions of politics, religion, ecology, technology, and human emotion, following young Paul Atreides as his family accepts the stewardship of the desert planet Arrakis—the exclusive source of the universe's most valuable substance, the spice melange.

