FINAL: The boiling point of a substance is the temperature at which the vapor pressure of the liquid equals the pressure surrounding the liquid and the liquid changes into vapor. 

### Key Characteristics
- **Pressure Dependence:** The boiling point of a liquid varies depending upon the surrounding environmental pressure. A liquid in a partial vacuum has a lower boiling point than when that liquid is at atmospheric pressure.
- **Normal Boiling Point:** The normal boiling point (also called the atmospheric boiling point or the atmospheric pressure boiling point) is a special case in which the vapor pressure of the liquid equals the defined atmospheric pressure at sea level, which is 1 atmosphere (101.325 kPa).
- **Phase Transition:** At the boiling point, molecules transition from the liquid phase to the gas phase. This process requires the addition of heat, known as the latent heat of vaporization, to overcome the intermolecular forces holding the liquid together.
- **Temperature Stability:** During the process of boiling, the temperature of the liquid remains constant until all of the liquid has been converted into gas, provided that the pressure remains constant.

### Factors Influencing Boiling Point
- **Intermolecular Forces:** Substances with stronger intermolecular forces (such as hydrogen bonding) generally have higher boiling points because more energy is required to transition the molecules into the gas phase.
- **Molecular Weight:** Generally, for similar types of molecules, those with higher molecular weights tend to have higher boiling points due to increased van der Waals forces.

Sources:
- [Boiling point](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Boiling_point)
